{"name":"Silver Lining","hex":"#b8b1a5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver lining","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A favorable aspect or prospect of a mostly unfavorable event or situation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_lining"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver lining (idiom)","blocks":[{"text":"A silver lining is a metaphor for optimism in vernacular English, which means a negative occurrence may have a positive aspect to it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/George_Lansbury_-_cartoon_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17653.png/330px-George_Lansbury_-_cartoon_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17653.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_lining_(idiom)"},{"title":"B. Reeves Eason","blocks":[{"text":"William Reeves Eason, known as B. Reeves Eason, was an American film director, actor and screenwriter. His directorial output was limited mainly to low-budget westerns and action pictures, but it was as a second-unit director and action specialist that he was best known. He was famous for staging spectacular battle scenes in war films and action scenes in large-budget westerns, but he acquired the nickname \"Breezy\" for his \"breezy\" attitude towards safety while staging his sequences—during the famous cavalry charge at the end of Charge of the Light Brigade (1936), so many horses were killed or injured so severely that they had to be euthanized that both the public and Hollywood itself were outraged, resulting in the selection of the American Humane Society by the beleaguered studios to provide representatives on the sets of all films using animals to ensure their safety."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/B_Reeves_Eason%2C_Lucien_Hubbard%2C_%26_Douglas_Z_Doty_-_Jan_1921_EH.jpg/330px-B_Reeves_Eason%2C_Lucien_Hubbard%2C_%26_Douglas_Z_Doty_-_Jan_1921_EH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B._Reeves_Eason"},{"title":"Silver Lining (Bonnie Raitt album)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Lining is the fourteenth studio album by Bonnie Raitt, released in 2002 on Capitol Records.\nThe album peaked at No. 13 on the US Billboard 200.\nSilver Lining has also been certified Gold in the US by the RIAA."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/Bonnie_Raitt_-_Silver_Lining.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Lining_(Bonnie_Raitt_album)"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg/500px-Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg/1280px-Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg/500px-Cloud_with_long_silver_lining.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p